; CARRY-1 (2026-09-19) — ROOM BOUNDS. The authored volume placement integrity layer 1 tests
; against (program doc §5b, a ruling from Talon): a prop may only be SET DOWN somewhere whose
; collision shape lies wholly inside one of these. It is the room's INTERIOR — floor top to
; ceiling underside, wall inner face to wall inner face — not the room's footprint, so a crate
; pushed half into a wall is outside it by construction.
;
; The GROUP is what PlacementIntegrity queries; the name is for whoever opens this scene looking
; for it. Groups go in the [node] header (.claude/rules/godot-scenes.md) — wired anywhere else
; they fail silently and look exactly like a feature that does nothing.
;
; It monitors nothing and is on no layer. It is a shape and a transform that the server reads
; directly; pairing it with every body that walks through it would be per-frame broadphase work
; for an answer nobody asks for.

[node name="RoomBounds" type="Area3D" parent="." groups=["room_bounds"]]
transform = Transform3D(1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 1.75, 0)
monitoring = false
monitorable = false
collision_layer = 0
collision_mask = 0

[node name="Collision" type="CollisionShape3D" parent="RoomBounds"]
shape = SubResource("BoxShape3D_bounds")

; BTN-1 (2026-09-19) — THE OBJECT RACK. The shelf of three near-misses the hider chooses from,
; and the reason the holding room exists as a room rather than as a lobby.
;
; SAME COLOUR, SAME SIZE CLASS, A SHAPE APART (program §0). The three objects are the ONLY thing
; either player learns about the target before the seek: the hider takes one, the other two stay
; here, and the seeker walks up and looks at what is left. That is why they are authored side by
; side at eye-line rather than in a bin — the shelf is a readout.
;
; THE IDS ARE THE NODE ORDER, AND THAT IS LOAD-BEARING TWICE. PropManager.AdoptAuthoredProps
; assigns authored ids by sorting on NODE PATH, identically on every peer, so Deal_0/1/2 here
; adopt as 1000/1001/1002 and the search room's four crates follow at 1003..1006. Two consequences
; a later lane must know:
;   - RENAMING one of these, or adding a fourth, RENUMBERS the search room's props. It already
;     did once: this rack is why tests/Run-PlaceTest.ps1's four prop ids moved from 1000..1003 to
;     1003..1006 (see that file's header and the BTN-1 handoff). SHELF-1's hundred will move them
;     again. A suite that hardcodes an authored prop id is a suite that breaks when a level grows.
;   - ObjectRack answers "is this one of mine" from its own CHILDREN, never from a literal, so
;     nothing in code has to be updated when they move.
;
; SHELF-1 REPLACES THE THREE scenes/game/props/Deal*.tscn FILES IN PLACE. Nothing in this scene
; changes when it does: the instance paths and the node names are the contract.
;
; y = 0.99 is the shelf's top surface (0.95 centre + 0.04 half-thickness). Each object's own
; half-height is added to that so it RESTS on the shelf rather than floating or intersecting —
; the can and the produce are 0.2 m tall (half 0.10) and the box is 0.26 m tall (half 0.13).

[node name="ObjectRack" type="Node3D" parent="." groups=["object_rack"]]
transform = Transform3D(1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, -4.6)
script = ExtResource("3_rack")

[node name="Shelf" type="StaticBody3D" parent="ObjectRack"]
transform = Transform3D(1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0.95, 0)

[node name="Collision" type="CollisionShape3D" parent="ObjectRack/Shelf"]
